    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S966\n\nSPONSOR: GAUGHRAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to direct the department of health and other state departments\nand agencies to conduct a study on the quality of life and human health\nimpacts of John F. Kennedy international airport and LaGuardia airport\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo require the Departments of Health and other departments deemed appro-\npriate by the governor, to conduct a study describing the environmental,\nnoise, and human health impacts caused by runways, including new\nrunways, and air traffic at John F. Kennedy International Airport and\nLaGuardia Airport.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill directs the Department of Health, with the assist-\nance of the Departments of Environmental Conservation and Transportation\nand other agencies if appropriate, to conduct a study describing the\nenvironmental, noise, and human health impacts caused by runways,\nincluding new runways, and air traffic at John F. Kennedy International\nAirport and LaGuardia Airport.\n\nThe bill mandates the monitoring of noise levels experienced in 2022 and\n2023 and that particular attention be paid to the impact of pollution\nand emissions on the life and health of residents living adjacent to\nflight paths at such airports. The Department of Health is required to\nreport its findings to the Governor and Legislature by March 31, 2024.\n\nSection 2 is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCommunities in Nassau and Suffolk counties and parts of New York City\nare experiencing a declining quality of life due to noise pollution,\nenvironmental impacts, and other possible ill effects from air traffic\npatterns around JFK and LaGuardia airports. Noise complaints in affected\nneighborhoods have skyrocketed as a result of changing air traffic\npatterns and too many planes flying on low approaches to these airports.\nThis has led to a near-constant and deafening noise level that studies\nhave shown is frequently above the day-night average noise level (DNL)\nof 65 decibels (dB), which is considered a significant aviation noise\nthreshold. Additionally, decibel levels are being averaged per day and\nthat does not truly characterize the excessive noise that the many\nplanes exceeding those threshold levels are making on a daily basis.\nThe averaging is deceptive and these measurement distortions should be\naddressed in the study.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2020: S7305/A10121\n2019: S5855/A7710 (Veto No. 228 - Similar bill)\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\n\nThere could be some financial cost to the agencies tasked with conduct-\ning these studies.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
