    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S966B\n\nSPONSOR: GAUGHRAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to direct the department of environmental conservation and other\nstate departments and agencies to conduct a study on the quality of life\nand human health impacts at John F. Kennedy international airport and\nLaGuardia airport\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo require the Department of Environmental Conservation, in consultation\nwith the Department of Health and other state agencies that the governor\ndeems appropriate, to conduct a study on the quality of life and human\nhealth impacts of John F. Kennedy International Airport and LaGuardia\nAirport.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill directs the Department of Environmental Conserva-\ntion, with the assistance of the Departments of Environmental Conserva-\ntion and Transportation and other agencies if appropriate, to conduct a\nstudy describing the environmental, noise, and human health impacts\ncaused by runways, including new runways, and air traffic at John F.\nKennedy International Airport and LaGuardia Airport. The bill mandates\nthe monitoring of noise levels experienced in 2022 and 2023 and that\nparticular attention be paid to the impact of pollution and emissions on\nthe life and health of residents living adjacent to flight paths at such\nairports. The Department of Health is required to report its findings to\nthe Governor and Legislature by March 31, 2024.\n\nThe bill includes $500,000 in appropriated funding from chapter 54 of\nthe laws of 2021 for such purposes.\n\nSection 2 is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCommunities in Nassau and Suffolk counties and parts of New York City\nare experiencing a declining quality of life due to noise pollution,\nenvironmental impacts, and other possible ill effects from air traffic\npatterns around JFK and LaGuardia airports. Noise complaints in affected\nneighborhoods have skyrocketed as a result of changing air traffic\npatterns and too many planes flying on low approaches to these airports.\nThis has led to a near-constant and deafening noise level that studies\nhave shown is frequently above the day-night average noise level (DNL)\nof 65 decibels (dB), which is considered a significant aviation noise\nthreshold. Additionally, decibel levels are being averaged per day and\nthat does not truly characterize the excessive noise that the many\nplanes exceeding those threshold levels are making on a daily basis. The\naveraging is deceptive and these measurement distortions should be\naddressed in the study.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2020: S7305/A10121\n\n2019: S5855/A7710 (Veto No. 228 - Similar bill)\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:  FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nThere could be some financial cost to the agencies tasked with conduct-\ning these studies. Funding may include $500,000 appropriated in chapter\n54 of the laws of 2021 for such purposes.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
